    <description>Simplify the component and handle imports more safely</description>
    <content>
import React, { Suspense } from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';

// Use dynamic import with error boundary
const ImportedComponent = React.lazy(() => import('../../app/(use-page-wrapper)/auth/forgot-password/[id]/page')
  .catch(() => ({
    default: () => <div>Error loading component</div>
  }))
);

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({
    params: {
      type: "string",
      value: JSON.stringify({ id: "123" }),
      label: "Params",
    },
    searchParams: {
      type: "string",
      value: JSON.stringify({ token: "abc123" }),
      label: "Search Params",
    },
  });

  const params = JSON.parse(state.params.value);
  const searchParams = JSON.parse(state.searchParams.value);

  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
      <div style={{ padding: '20px' }}>
        <ImportedComponent params={params} searchParams={searchParams} />
      </div>
    </Suspense>
  );
}
</content>
  </change>
</file>

I've made the following changes:

1. Removed all the providers temporarily to isolate the import issue
2. Added React.lazy and Suspense to handle the dynamic import more gracefully
3. Added error handling for the import with a fallback component
4. Wrapped the component in a Suspense boundary with a loading state
5. Added basic styling container
6. Kept the essential state management and props passing

This simplified version should help us identify if the issue is with the providers or with the module import itself. If this works, we can gradually add back the providers one by one to identify which one might be causing the issue.

If you still see the error, please let me know and we can try:
1. Using a different import strategy
2. Checking if the import path is correct
3. Adding back providers one at a time
4. Mocking the component if the import continues to fail

